<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!-- eno brand tokens (src/app/globals.css :root). styles.xml referenced these names before
         this file existed, so they silently resolved to the Capacitor library's Indigo defaults.
         Dark values live in values-night/colors.xml — keep both files in sync with globals.css. -->
    <color name="colorPrimary">#0a66c2</color><!-- brand -->
    <color name="colorPrimaryDark">#034078</color><!-- brand-dark -->
    <color name="colorAccent">#0a66c2</color><!-- brand -->
    <!-- card: the web chrome surface. Used as splash + window background so cold start and any
         system-bar strip the window paints match the page instead of flashing white/black.

         ⛔ THIS HAD DRIFTED FROM globals.css AND THE PULL-TO-REFRESH SHOWED IT. Owner, 2026-09-06:
         "pulling down cuts top and screen with white in between" — a strip of the wrong colour
         above the header on every pull.
         ⚠️ AND NOT FOR THE REASON THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS COMMENT GAVE. It said SwipeRefreshLayout
         drags the WebView down and reveals the window behind it. It does not: SwipeRefreshLayout.java
         onLayout lays the child at `getPaddingTop()` unconditionally and moves only the spinner
         circle. What actually uncovers the window colour is the WebView's own overscroll. The fix
         is the same either way — the window must not be a colour the page never uses — but the
         mechanism had to be corrected rather than left as a plausible story.
         Measured, not guessed: globals.css:250 var(background) #fafafa, :261 var(card) #fafafa,
         :565 var(background) #1b1b1b, :569 var(card) #1b1b1b. Keep all four in step. -->
    <color name="card">#fafafa</color>
    <!-- ⛔ THE PUCK IS WHITE IN BOTH THEMES, AND THERE IS NO values-night OVERRIDE FOR IT ON PURPOSE.
         It had one (#242424) and that CREATED a regression three reviewers kept returning to: every
         colour in this file resolves from ANDROID's night mode, while the web app in the WebView has
         its own switch (html.t-light / t-dark). A user who explicitly picks Light in-app on a Dark
         phone would have got a #242424 puck over a light page — worse than the platform default
         they had before. Dropping the night value removes the whole class: white is what Android
         ships, it is readable on #fafafa and on #1b1b1b alike, and it cannot be wrong for either
         override. Neither of the owner's actual complaints depended on it — the grey ARC is fixed
         by refreshSpinner below, and the white BAND by `card` above.
         ⚠️ `card` STILL FOLLOWS ANDROID'S NIGHT MODE and so still mismatches an in-app override.
         That one is inherited, not introduced here, and closing it needs a real theme bridge (web
         preference -> Preferences -> AppCompatDelegate.setDefaultNightMode plus a window repaint).
         Do not "fix" either by inventing a compromise colour that is wrong in both themes. -->
    <color name="refreshSpinner">#0a66c2</color><!-- brand arc, instead of the platform's grey -->
    <color name="refreshSpinnerBg">#ffffff</color><!-- see above: deliberately theme-independent -->

    <!-- ⛔ BOTH THEMES' CANVAS, SPELLED OUT HERE RATHER THAN SPLIT ACROSS values/ AND values-night/,
         SO JAVA CAN PICK. `card` above still exists for styles.xml windowBackground, which the
         framework resolves before any web code has run and which therefore has to follow Android.
         Everything AFTER first paint follows the APP's theme instead, read from the native
         Preferences mirror — see MainActivity.appIsDark(). Keep these two in step with globals.css
         var(background)/var(card): #fafafa light, #1b1b1b dark. -->
    <color name="cardLight">#fafafa</color>
    <color name="cardDark">#1b1b1b</color>
    <color name="refreshSpinnerLight">#0a66c2</color><!-- brand -->
    <color name="refreshSpinnerDark">#3b8ee6</color><!-- brand, brightened for dark surfaces -->
</resources>
